Below is a compact, but complete, C# console‑app that shows **all the ways you usually work with nullable value types** (`Nullable<T>`, the syntactic sugar `int?`, null‑checks, the null‑coalescing operator, pattern matching, and even a nullable enum).  
Feel free to paste it into a new `Program.cs` file in a .NET 8 (or newer) console project and run it – the output demonstrates what each construct does.

```csharp
using System;

namespace NullableDemo
{
    // A tiny enum so we can show a nullable enum as well.
    enum Status
    {
        Pending,
        Approved,
        Rejected
    }

    class Program
    {
        static void Main()
        {
            // 1. Value‑type nullable (int?) -------------------------------------------------
            int? maybeNumber = null;          // no value
            PrintNullableInt("maybeNumber (null)", maybeNumber);

            maybeNumber = 42;                 // has a value
            PrintNullableInt("maybeNumber (42)", maybeNumber);

            // 2. Working with Nullable<T> explicitly ------------------------------------------
            Nullable<double> maybeDouble = new Nullable<double>(3.1415);
            if (maybeDouble.HasValue)        // HasValue is true
                Console.WriteLine($"maybeDouble: {maybeDouble.Value}");
            else
                Console.WriteLine("maybeDouble has no value.");

            maybeDouble = null;              // reset to null
            Console.WriteLine($"maybeDouble after reset: {(maybeDouble.HasValue ? maybeDouble.Value.ToString() : "null")}");

            // 3. Null‑coalescing operator to provide a default -------------------------------
            int numberOrDefault = maybeNumber ?? -1;   // if null, use -1
            Console.WriteLine($"numberOrDefault (using ??): {numberOrDefault}");

            // 4. Pattern matching (C# 7+), useful in switch or if statements
            if (maybeNumber is int val)
                Console.WriteLine($"maybeNumber contains: {val}");
            else
                Console.WriteLine("maybeNumber is null.");

            // 5. Nullable enum ---------------------------------------------------------------
            Status? maybeStatus = Status.Approved;
            switch (maybeStatus)
            {
                case Status.Pending:
                    Console.WriteLine("Status: Pending");
                    break;
                case Status.Approved:
                    Console.WriteLine("Status: Approved");
                    break;
                case Status.Rejected:
                    Console.WriteLine("Status: Rejected");
                    break;
                case null:
                    Console.WriteLine("Status: (no status)");
                    break;
            }

            // 6. Nullable struct example (DateTime?) -----------------------------------------
            DateTime? maybeDate = DateTime.Today;
            Console.WriteLine($"maybeDate: {maybeDate?.ToShortDateString() ?? "no date"}");

            maybeDate = null;
            Console.WriteLine($"maybeDate after reset: {maybeDate?.ToShortDateString() ?? "no date"}");
        }

        // Helper method to demonstrate null‑checks for int?
        static void PrintNullableInt(string label, int? value)
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"{label} => {(value.HasValue ? value.Value.ToString() : "null")}");
        }
    }
}
```

### What the code does

| # | Feature | How it’s shown |
|---|---------|----------------|
| 1 | `int?` (nullable value type) | Declared, assigned `null`, then a real value. |
| 2 | `Nullable<T>` explicitly | `Nullable<double>` used instead of the sugar `double?`. |
| 3 | Null‑coalescing `??` | Provide a default value when the nullable is `null`. |
| 4 | Pattern matching | `is int val` extracts the underlying value if present. |
| 5 | Nullable enum | `Status?` used in a `switch` with a `case null:` clause. |
| 6 | Nullable struct (`DateTime?`) | Show how to safely format a nullable date with the null‑conditional operator `?.` and another `??` for a fallback string. |

### Running the program

When you run the application you’ll see output something like:

```
maybeNumber (null) => null
maybeNumber (42) => 42
maybeDouble: 3.1415
maybeDouble after reset: null
numberOrDefault (using ??): 42
maybeNumber contains: 42
Status: Approved
maybeDate: 10/31/2026
maybeDate after reset: no date
```

The output confirms that the nullable types behave exactly as expected: they can hold either a value or `null`, you can test for presence with `HasValue` or pattern matching, and you can supply defaults or fallback values with the `??` operator. This snippet should give you a solid foundation for working with nullable value types in any C# project.
